Easy parallelism. Works well for CPU-bound, side-effect-free work over large data. Don't blindly add parallel() to small lists or IO-bound work — overhead can outweigh the benefit. Measure.
Trick: the modern terminal .toList() (Java 16+) replaces .collect(Collectors.toList()) for the common case. Less typing; returns an unmodifiable list.

A small worked example tying chapters 12-14:
record Sale(String region, String product, double amount) {}
List<Sale> sales = List.of(
new Sale("APAC", "Apple", 120.0),
new Sale("APAC", "Banana", 80.0),
new Sale("EMEA", "Apple", 200.0),
new Sale("EMEA", "Cherry", 60.0),
new Sale("APAC", "Apple", 150.0)
);
// total per region
Map<String, Double> byRegion = sales.stream()
.collect(groupingBy(Sale::region, summingDouble(Sale::amount)));
// {APAC=350.0, EMEA=260.0}
// top product per region
Map<String, String> topProduct = sales.stream()
.collect(groupingBy(
Sale::region,
collectingAndThen(
maxBy(Comparator.comparingDouble(Sale::amount)),
o -> o.map(Sale::product).orElse(null))));
// {APAC=Apple, EMEA=Apple}
In a few declarative lines: grouping + aggregation + selection. Same logic in classic loops would be 30+ lines and harder to verify.
